{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If a man vows a vow to HaShem.</b> There are two kinds of vows. For one who vows in his anger to forbid upon himself some thing — this vow is not 'to HaShem,' and specifically 'if a man vows' — when he is a man settled in his mind, we apply to him 'the man in the oath' (Shevuot 26a), and he is the one who vows 'to HaShem.' But one who vows in his anger — since 'anger rests in the bosom of fools' (Ecclesiastes 7:9), if so he is not a 'man' and is likened to a beast. And the Sages (Nedarim 22a) spoke in disparagement of one who vows and said that one who vows is as though he built a bamah, and one who fulfills it is as though he offered upon it. And as a proof for their words: for Rashi explained, 'זֶה הַדָּבָר' is said here, and 'זֶה הַדָּבָר' is said in the case of slaughter outside the Temple, etc. And I say: learn from this and from that — just as there it concerns a bamah, so too here it concerns a bamah; for slaughter outside the Temple means one who offers on a bamah, so too 'זֶה הַדָּבָר' said here concerning one who vows speaks of one who offers on a bamah, for one who vows is like one who built a bamah. And the reason for this is that the Torah descended to the mindset of the one who vows: since he forbids upon himself what the whole world treats as permitted, presumably the haughtiness of his heart moved him to separate himself from the entire congregation and set himself apart from the community." "And in this manner we find in the language of the Sages (Chagigah 22b): 'let not each person go and build a bamah for himself' — for all this is a path of pride and arrogance, as the Sages said (Sotah 4b): whoever has arrogance is as though he built a bamah, as it says (Isaiah 2:22): 'Cease from man, in whose nostrils is a breath — כִּי בַמֶּה נֶחְשָׁב הוּא' — do not read 'בַּמֶּה' (for what) but 'בָּמָה' (bamah). What does arrogance have to do with building a bamah? But surely it is that on account of his arrogance he builds a bamah for himself, as stated. And likewise 'bamah' is a term for height, and that path of pride and arrogance found in one who builds a bamah is found also in one who vows, for presumably his intention is so that he be regarded as a pietist and ascetic. But there is room to say: let one who vows in his anger be the proof, for presumably in his anger he did not intend for self-aggrandizement, and you must distinguish between one who vows from settled mind and one who vows in his anger. And by what will you know whether this particular vow-maker resembles one who builds a bamah or not? For if he vowed in his anger, he surely does not resemble one who builds a bamah. On this they said: 'and one who fulfills it is as though he offered upon it' — they gave a clear sign for this: whatever a person does out of anger, presumably when his wrath subsides he comes to regret it and does not fulfill it, but rather goes to the sage to have it annulled. Therefore they said: if he vows and does not fulfill it but rather seeks release from his vow, he surely vowed in anger and regrets it, and then he does not resemble one who built a bamah. And as the Sages said (Chagigah 10a): 'שֶׁנִּשְׁבַּעְתִּי בְאַפִּי' etc. — in My anger I swore, and I retracted when the anger and wrath subsided." "But one who fulfills it and does not come to regret it and does not seek release from it — his end proves that he vowed from settled mind, and the Torah suspected him that the haughtiness of his heart brought him to this trait of taking himself to another direction; therefore he is like one who offered upon it, for the fulfillment is the completion of his intent, just as the offering is the purpose of building the bamah. And this is what they conclude there (Nedarim 22a): Samuel said: all who vow are called wicked. What is the verse? As it says (Deuteronomy 23:23): 'וְכִי תֶחְדַּל לִנְדֹּר לֹא יִהְיֶה בְךָ חֵטְא.' And we learn a verbal analogy of חֲדָלָה / חֲדָלָה: it is written 'וְכִי תֶחְדַּל,' and it is written there (Job 3:17): 'שָׁם רְשָׁעִים חָדְלוּ רֹגֶז.' And it appears to me to say: learn from this and from that — just as there it speaks of the cessation of rage, so too here it speaks of the cessation of rage. For he is called wicked only in this manner: that his rage has already ceased and nevertheless he stands by his vow and does not seek release from it — it is thereby revealed retroactively that he did not vow out of anger, for a span of time equal to the cessation of rage has already elapsed. And concerning this it says: 'שָׁם רְשָׁעִים' — meaning, he is called wicked when the rage has ceased and he does not seek release from it; and if so, he surely vowed from settled mind in order to build a bamah for himself; therefore one who fulfills it is called wicked, for it is a path of pride and arrogance to him. And I add yet another verbal analogy, to learn this חֲדָלָה from 'חִדְלוּ לָכֶם מִן הָאָדָם אֲשֶׁר נְשָׁמָה בְּאַפּוֹ כִּי בַמֶּה נֶחְשָׁב' — just as there it concerns a bamah, so too here it concerns a bamah. And this is what it says here: 'אִישׁ כִּי יִדֹּר נֶדֶר לַה׳' — when he is a 'man' in his vow and clear-minded, and he vowed from settled mind — concerning him it says by way of declaration: 'כְּכָל הַיֹּצֵא מִפִּיו יַעֲשֶׂה,' for his end will not be to come to regret. But one who vows out of anger — he declares but does not fulfill, for his vow will ultimately be annulled when his wrath subsides. And it says 'a vow to HaShem' to exclude one who does not vow for any self-aggrandizement to build a bamah for himself, but rather vows for the sake of HaShem." "<b>He shall not profane his word.</b> But others may profane his word. The reason for the annulment of vows by a singular expert or by three laymen who are called a court — we learn this from the annulment by the father and the husband. For just as the father and the husband have it in their power to annul, because every woman is under the authority of her husband or under the authority of her father, and she has no power to do anything great or small without their agreement, and it is as though they stipulated at the time of the vow on condition that the husband or the father agree — so when they do not agree, the vow is retroactively nullified from its root, for presumably she vowed with their consent, or as though she vowed with their consent — so too every man of Israel is under the authority of the court and obligated to do all that the court instructs him, and every vow-maker is as though he stipulated at the time of the vow on condition that the court agree with him; and when they do not agree, the vow is uprooted from its root. And every singular expert is called a court, and any three laymen; therefore it is in their power to annul his vow to prevent him from sin, for every vow-maker is called a sinner, from what is said (Deuteronomy 23:23): 'וְכִי תֶחְדַּל לִנְדֹּר לֹא יִהְיֶה בְךָ חֵטֵא.' And the reason that the husband and the father use the language of הֲפָרָה (annulment/uprooting) while the sage uses the language of הַתָּרָה (release) — is because a woman is more under the authority of her husband and her father than all of Israel are under the authority of the court; therefore they use language indicating nullification of the vow from its root, as though there had been no vow at all. But the language of הַתָּרָה indicates a matter that was forbidden at first for some time, and that prohibition was released — for it is not uprooted entirely, since the matter depends on regret (see Rambam, Laws of Vows 13:15, and the Radbaz there)."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The phrase שחוטי חוץ refers to sacrifices slaughtered outside the Temple, a cardinal prohibition; Rashi on Numbers 30:3 cites the verbal analogy between 'זֶה הַדָּבָר' here and in Leviticus 17:2ff., establishing the link to bamot. The Kli Yakar's abbreviated 'וכו׳' marks where Rashi's chain continues.", 1 "The verse quoted from Psalms 95:11 (cited in Chagigah 10a) appears in the Hebrew text here as 'אשר נשבעתי באפי'; the standard text of Chagigah 10a cites it with the divine name, which the Kli Yakar or the typesetter may have abbreviates or paraphrased. Translated hyper-literally as it stands. The phrase 'נודר בכעסו יוכיח' is rendered as 'let one who vows in his anger be the proof.'", 2 "The reading 'בחדלת הרגז משתעי' is Aramaic for 'it speaks of' (שׁתע), standard Talmudic formula in verbal analogies; rendered accordingly.", 3 "The lemma 'לֹא יַחֵל דְּבָרוֹ' (Numbers 30:3) literally means 'he shall not profane his word'; the Kli Yakar plays on the contrast: he himself may not profane it, but others (a court or expert) may profane/release it for him. The terms הֲפָרָה and הַתָּרָה are technically distinct in halakhic literature: the former (used for father/husband) uproots the vow retroactively as if it never existed; the latter (used by a sage) releases a prohibition that was in effect for a time. Rambam, Laws of Vows 13:15, and Radbaz ad loc., discuss whether hatarah depends on the development of regret (חֲרָטָה) at the time of annulment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:57.358656166Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}